# L0441: an `interrupt fn` declared in a HOSTED module of a mixed-tier build.
#
# This file carries no `no-runtime` directive, so `hosted_isr` is a safe-tier
# declaration and the hardware calling convention is unavailable to it — even
# though the program it belongs to *does* contain a freestanding module
# (`kernel.lby`), which it imports.
#
# The tier is a **module** property, and a multi-file build is merged into one
# flat program before semantic analysis runs. A gate that consulted only the
# merged program's whole-unit `is_no_runtime` flag would get this wrong in both
# directions — and did, transiently, when this feature was rebased onto the
# per-module tier attribution work. See the valid counterpart,
# `tests/fixtures/valid/modules_freestanding_isr/`, which pins the other
# direction: a legitimate ISR in a freestanding module of a program whose entry
# file is hosted must still be ACCEPTED.
import kernel

interrupt fn hosted_isr frame ptr<i64>
    unsafe
        ptr_write(frame, 1)

fn main -> i64
    kernel_answer()
